  "interpretation": "The signed runtime is the condition in which E/I organization can register at all. Its spectral radius separates the real graph from the probe's weak null by a factor of 3.6 (0.8289 against 0.2298), where the unsigned runtime separates them by a factor of 1.003 because incoming-L1 normalization of non-negative weights pins the radius near 1 for any topology. Signing does not fix cross-action memory: the half-life is still two actions, and the lower spectral radius makes absolute retention shorter than the unsigned condition at the same gain. The weak null is not the preregistered directed degree-preserving null, and no biological-advantage claim rests on it.",
